1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,206
Bulk buying has a pretty simple strategy.

2
00:00:03,206 --> 00:00:06,187
Make one person buy a
ridiculous amount of something.

3
00:00:06,687 --> 00:00:08,907
Which is great... if I need twenty-four.

4
00:00:10,306 --> 00:00:14,193
Pool flips that. Instead of finding
one person who wants twenty-four —

5
00:00:14,544 --> 00:00:17,344
what if you found twenty-four
people who each want one?

6
00:00:18,244 --> 00:00:23,440
On a campus, thousands of us live
within walking distance, buying the same stuff anyway.

7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,437
Protein powder. Snacks. Drinks. Toiletries.

8
00:00:28,437 --> 00:00:31,061
The discount is not the hard part. This is.

9
00:00:31,462 --> 00:00:34,831
You have to find everyone,
work out what they will accept,

10
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:38,200
collect the money, receive the
shipment, organise pickup —

11
00:00:38,449 --> 00:00:40,802
and hope nobody disappears halfway through.

12
00:00:41,502 --> 00:00:43,989
So basically nobody wants to organise it.

13
00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:46,596
So I built Pool.

14
00:00:48,496 --> 00:00:51,287
The whole idea is that
I do not create a group.

15
00:00:51,287 --> 00:00:53,721
I just tell Pool something I already buy.

16
00:00:54,421 --> 00:00:58,376
I normally get this Kestrel medium
roast. Three bags at a time.

17
00:00:59,476 --> 00:01:02,816
I would take another brand — but
Pool is not allowed to guess that.

18
00:01:03,716 --> 00:01:05,995
So I allow alternatives explicitly.

19
00:01:05,995 --> 00:01:10,162
Still whole bean, still caffeinated,
and dark roast is fine too.

20
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:11,674
Done.

21
00:01:13,574 --> 00:01:15,242
And this is the important part.

22
00:01:16,742 --> 00:01:21,847
I never created a group. I did not invite
anybody. I did not choose a supplier.

23
00:01:22,447 --> 00:01:24,916
And I definitely did not pick Harbourstone.

24
00:01:25,816 --> 00:01:28,659
Pool found five other people
whose demand fits mine,

25
00:01:28,659 --> 00:01:31,120
and formed an eighteen-bag
provisional order.

26
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:36,101
My three bags go from fifty-five fifty
buying alone, to about forty-four.

27
00:01:37,601 --> 00:01:39,827
And the obvious choice actually loses.

28
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:43,230
Kestrel had more demand. Twenty-three bags.

29
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:46,537
But together it would have
cost three sixty-seven,

30
00:01:46,537 --> 00:01:48,594
against three sixty buying separately.

31
00:01:49,194 --> 00:01:50,567
So... terrible group deal.

32
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:54,565
Pool rejects it, and costs
another compatible option.

33
00:01:55,065 --> 00:02:00,913
Harbourstone works. Three complete cases,
nothing left over, sixty-nine dollars saved.

34
00:02:02,313 --> 00:02:05,094
And sometimes the right
answer is just... no.

35
00:02:05,794 --> 00:02:09,939
I also buy paper towels. Pool
finds seven packs of demand nearby.

36
00:02:09,939 --> 00:02:12,234
The supplier minimum is forty-eight.

37
00:02:13,034 --> 00:02:16,705
So, no fake deal. No forced group. It waits.

38
00:02:18,205 --> 00:02:20,431
And these preferences are not suggestions.

39
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:22,844
If I change this to Kestrel only,

40
00:02:22,844 --> 00:02:26,035
Pool takes my units back out
of the Harbourstone order.

41
00:02:26,635 --> 00:02:30,124
It re-forms without me. It would
rather leave me out than buy me

42
00:02:30,124 --> 00:02:31,868
something I said I did not want.

43
00:02:33,168 --> 00:02:34,987
And nobody here created a group.

44
00:02:35,487 --> 00:02:39,087
These are fifty-one independent
things people already said they buy.

45
00:02:39,087 --> 00:02:41,151
Pool is the thing noticing the overlap.

46
00:02:42,251 --> 00:02:43,913
Then there is the local part.

47
00:02:43,913 --> 00:02:48,670
A neighbour hosts the order, collects the
shipment, runs the pickup, and gets paid.

48
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:52,864
In this prototype, purchasing
and payments are simulated.

49
00:02:52,864 --> 00:02:54,889
Nothing has actually been charged.

50
00:02:57,489 --> 00:03:00,458
Underneath, we made one
decision that matters a lot.

51
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:05,639
The agent decides what is worth
investigating, and what to try next.

52
00:03:06,338 --> 00:03:09,320
Ordinary deterministic
code decides what is true —

53
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:13,376
the people, the quantities, the
prices, and whether the order works.

54
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:16,625
So the AI gets freedom to coordinate.

55
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:19,397
It does not get freedom to make up the math.

56
00:03:21,297 --> 00:03:23,654
The coordination loop is built with Strands.

57
00:03:24,154 --> 00:03:26,761
The public demo runs a
deterministic planner,

58
00:03:26,761 --> 00:03:29,716
so any judge can reproduce
it at zero model tokens.

59
00:03:30,216 --> 00:03:34,740
And the same bounded agent is separately
deployed on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore,

60
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:35,610
with Nova Lite.

61
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:41,705
We still need live supplier inventory and pricing
before any of this is a real purchase.

62
00:03:42,105 --> 00:03:43,828
But the idea is pretty simple.

63
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:49,031
Costco gives one person the purchasing
power of twenty-four buyers.

64
00:03:49,531 --> 00:03:52,335
Pool gives twenty-four
people that purchasing power,

65
00:03:52,335 --> 00:03:55,032
without making anyone buy
twenty-four of anything.

66
00:03:56,632 --> 00:03:58,159
Nobody organised the group.

67
00:03:59,060 --> 00:03:59,859
Pool noticed.
